analysis:
This PR is documentation-only (docs/architecture/hardware-support.md and docs/architecture/inference.md). It changes no code, keys, endpoints, attestation flow or encryption path, so it adds no security control and removes none. I found no concrete regression.

The inference.md patch (hunk at line 68) adds a GLM5Next implementation-boundary section. It describes:
- catalog-only `glm-5.3-flash` serving, with an identity chain (catalog entry, revision receipt, per-file manifest hash) and a shipped-template check;
- an M5 Max/Ultra with at least 256 GiB hardware requirement;
- fail-closed retained-owner behaviour when native retirement is unproven;
- quarantine of GLM work only, with unpublished bytes charged into every fleet KV budget;
- media requests excluded from prefix lookup and capture (no cross-image KV reuse), and video refused with 400;
- encrypted checkpoint storage identity including executionPolicy.

Relevant threats: T-041 (prefix cache), T-027/T-012 (weight and artifact substitution), T-010/T-029 (DoS and resource exhaustion), T-028 (residual GPU data), T-056.

Two observations for later passes, neither raised as a finding:
- The doc admits that step scratch is not pre-reserved at admission, so a full ledger can fail an already-admitted request mid-step. This is a documented deferral, not a new vulnerability. A later pass should confirm that high concurrency of long-context GLM requests cannot be used by a consumer as a DoS vector.
- Retained GLM faults charge unpublished weight and KV bytes into every family's KV budget. A later pass should check whether a consumer can trigger a native-retirement fault.

hardware-support.md is supplied as head text only, with no patch. It is consistent with the existing memory-admission model (hardCapBytes, process ledger) and shows no weakened invariants. Source code was not supplied, so claims in the docs are unverified against it.
